[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[manpage_begin "Event log" n ""]
[copyright "2004-2026, Ashok P. Nadkarni"]
[moddesc "Event Log"]
[titledesc "Commands relating to reading and writing the Windows event log"]
[require twapi_eventlog]
[description]
[para]

The [cmd twapi_evt] package implements commands for reading and writing to
the Windows Event log.

[section Sessions]

An event log [emph session] is a connection to the event log service on
a system. Most commands for reading and writing event logs and related
configuration work within the context of a session which defaults to
the local system. The following commands are used to managed sessions.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_session_close [cmd evt_session_close]]]
Closes a session to an event log service.
[opt_def [uri #evt_session_is_local [cmd evt_session_is_local]]]
Checks if a session is local.
[opt_def [uri #evt_session_open [cmd evt_session_open]]]
Opens a session to a remote or local event log service using passed
credentials.
[opt_def [uri #evt_session_open_local [cmd evt_session_open_local]]]
Returns the session to the local event log service
using current credentials.
[list_end]

[section Publishers]

An event [emph publisher], or [emph provider] (the Windows SDK uses the term
interchangeably) defines the structure of events it may generate, the channels
to which it writes events, and localized message strings. This structure is
described by metadata in a publisher manifest that is installed on the system.

[para]
The following commands return information
related to publishers.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_events [cmd evt_publisher_events]]]
Returns the metadata for the events defined by a publisher.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_message [cmd evt_publisher_message]]]
Returns the message corresponding to a message id from the publisher's
message resource.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_open [cmd evt_publisher_open]]]
Returns a handle to a publisher's metadata.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_property [cmd evt_publisher_property]]]
Returns the value of a publisher metadata property.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_install [cmd evt_publisher_install]]]
Installs a publisher to the local system.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publisher_uninstall [cmd evt_publisher_uninstall]]]
Uninstalls a publisher from the local system.
[opt_def [uri #evt_publishers [cmd evt_publishers]]]
Returns a list of publishers installed on the local system.
[opt_def [uri #evt_twapi_install [cmd evt_twapi_install]]]
Installs the TWAPI event publisher.
[opt_def [uri #evt_twapi_uninstall [cmd evt_twapi_uninstall]]]
Uninstalls the TWAPI event publisher.
[list_end]

[section Channels]

Event log [emph channels] are named event streams from which events are
read or written. Each channel has configuration properties that control
aspects of channel behavior. The related commands are shown in the table
below.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_channel_config_get [cmd evt_channel_config_get]]]
Retrieves a channel configuration property value.
[opt_def [uri #evt_channel_config_open [cmd evt_channel_config_open]]]
Open the configuration property database for a channel.
[opt_def [uri #evt_channel_config_save [cmd evt_channel_config_save]]]
Save all modified channel configuration properties.
[opt_def [uri #evt_channel_config_set [cmd evt_channel_config_set]]]
Sets a channel configuration property value.
[opt_def [uri #evt_channels [cmd evt_channels]]]
Returns the list of channel names.
[list_end]

[section "Consuming events"]

Event consumers can consume events from channels in real time, or from a saved
event file, by calling [uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]] and iterating over
the returned result set with [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]]. The following commands
return the data present in an event in various forms.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_decode [cmd evt_event_decode]]]
Returns a dictionary mapping event fields names to their corresponding
values with message ids formatted as localized strings.
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_decode_list [cmd evt_event_decode_list]]]
Similar to [cmd evt_event_decode] but takes multiple events and returns
a [uri base.html#recordarray [cmd recordarray]].
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_logpath [cmd evt_event_logpath]]]
Returns the channel or file from which the event was read.
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_message [cmd evt_event_message]]]
Returns the formatted message for the event.
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]]]
Returns event fields as a list based on a rendering context. Does not
map message ids or format messages.
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_xml [cmd evt_event_xml]]]
Returns the event fields in XML format. Does not include rendered
messages. Use the [cmd -xml] option to
[uri #evt_event_decode [cmd evt_event_decode]] if that is desired.

[list_end]

The [uri #evt_event_decode [cmd evt_event_decode]] and
[uri #evt_event_decode_list [cmd evt_event_decode_list]] commands
support mapping of message ids to localized strings and formatting of
messages. The [uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]] and
[uri #evt_event_xml [cmd evt_event_xml]] commands
do not perform the above mapping or formatting.

[para]
The [cmd evt_event_render] command requires a
[emph "render context"] to be passed which selects the event properties
that are to be rendered from an event. The following commands create
a render context.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_system [cmd evt_render_context_system]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for system-defined fields.
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_userdata [cmd evt_render_context_userdata]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for publisher-defined fields.
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_xpaths [cmd evt_render_context_xpaths]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for fields matching
XPATH expressions.
[list_end]

[section Bookmarks]
An EVT [emph bookmark] identifies a specific event in an event log. Bookmarks are
created with [uri #evt_bookmark_create [cmd evt_bookmark_create]] and
set to point to a specific event either by passing a
XML representation previously generated with
[uri #evt_bookmark_render [cmd evt_bookmark_render]] to that command or
with [uri #evt_bookmark_update [cmd evt_bookmark_update]] when given
a event handle. A bookmark may be used to resume processing from its
targeted event in an event query by passing it to
[uri #evt_seek [cmd evt_seek]] or [uri #evt_subscribe [cmd evt_subscribe]].

[para]
All handles returned by the [cmd evt_*] commands, such as those returned from
[uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]] and [uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]]
must be closed by passing them to [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]].

[para]
Event logs can be exported to file using one of the following functions.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_archive_exported_log [cmd evt_archive_exported_log]]]
Archives an exported event log file with localized strings.
[opt_def [uri #evt_export_log [cmd evt_export_log]]]
Exports a event log channel to a log file.
[list_end]

[para]
To monitor the event log in real time, use the
[uri #evt_subscribe [cmd evt_subscribe]] command.

[section "Commands"]

[para]
[emph {
    Many commands dealing with the event log require the process to be running
    with administrative privileges and/or in elevated mode.
}]


[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd evt_archive_exported_log] [arg LOGPATH] [opt [arg options]]]
Archives the exported event log in file [arg LOGPATH]
along with formatting information so
that it can be viewed on systems where the event publishers are not installed.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]]
The locale identifier to use for the formatting messages in the archive.
[opt_def [cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]]
Specifies the EVT session handle. Defaults to the local system.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_bookmark_create] [opt [arg XML]]]
Creates and returns a handle to an event bookmark. A bookmark identifies a
specific event in an event log and may be used to restart event processing from
that event at a later time.
If [arg XML] is specified, it must be the XML representation of a
bookmark as previously returned by
[uri #evt_bookmark_render [cmd evt_bookmark_render]].
[nl]
The returned bookmark identifies the event represented by the XML. If [arg XML]
is an empty string, the returned bookmark is not associated with any event and
may subsequently be updated using [cmd evt_bookmark_update].
[nl]
The returned handle should be released via [cmd evt_close].

[call [cmd evt_bookmark_render] [arg HBOOOKMARK]]
Returns the XML form of a bookmark. This can then be passed to the
[uri #evt_bookmark_create [cmd evt_bookmark_create]] command to
recreate the bookmark to an event at some later time.

[call [cmd evt_bookmark_update] [arg HBOOKMARK] [arg HEVENT]]

Updates the bookmark specified by [arg HBOOKMARK] to identify the event
specified by [arg HEVENT]. [arg HBOOKMARK] and [arg HEVENT] must be
bookmark and event handles returned by
[uri #evt_bookmark_create [cmd evt_bookmark_create]]
and [uri #evt_next [cmd uri_next]] respectively.
The event handle must refer to an event returned by [cmd evt_next]. The
bookmark may subsequently be used with commands like
[uri #evt_seek [cmd evt_seek]] or [uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]]
to resume processing from the bookmarked event.



[call [cmd evt_channel_config_get] [arg HCHANCFG] [arg PROPERTY]]
Returns the value of a configuration property [arg PROPERTY] for the channel
configuration specified by the handle [arg HCHANCFG].
See [uri #evt_channel_config_open [cmd evt_channel_config_open]] for
a list of properties.

[call [cmd evt_channel_config_open] [arg CHANNELNAME] [opt "[cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]"]]
Returns a handle to the configuration of the named channel. Opens the
configuration for an event log channel and returns a handle to it.
[cmd -session] specifies the handle for the EVT session for the channel's
host which defaults to the local system. The returned handle may be used with
[uri #evt_channel_config_get [cmd evt_channel_config_get]] and
[uri #evt_channel_config_set [cmd evt_channel_config_set]]
to query and modify the channel configuration. When longer needed,
it should be released with [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]].

[nl]
The configuration properties for channels are managed through options
to the
[uri #evt_channel_config_get [cmd evt_channel_config_get]]
[uri #evt_channel_config_set [cmd evt_channel_config_set]]
commands. These options and the corresponding
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-schema "event manifest schema"]
elements or attributes are shown in the table below.

[list_begin opt]

[opt_def -access]
An SDDL access descriptor that controls access to the channel and the backing
store in conjunction with the [cmd -isolation] property.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.access].

[opt_def -autobackup]
[const 1] if a new log file is created when the current backing file reaches its
maximum size and [const 0] otherwise. In the former case, [cmd -retention] must
have a true value.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.retention].

[opt_def -buffersize]
Memory buffer size for the channel in Kilobytes.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.bufferSize].

[opt_def -classic]
[const 1] or [const 0] indicating whether the channel is a classic (pre-Vista)
channel or not.

[opt_def -clocktype]
[const 0] if using system time for time stamps and [const 1] for QPC.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.clockType].

[opt_def -controlguid]
Session GUID for a ETW session. Generally not present for event log channels.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.controlGuid].

[opt_def -enabled]
[const 1] or [const 0] depending on whether the channel is enabled or not.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.enabled].

[opt_def -filemax]
Maximum number of times a new log file should be created when the channel is
enabled including reboots.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.fileMax].

[opt_def -isolation]
One of [const 0] (Application), [const 1] (System) or [const 2] (Custom).
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.isolation].

[opt_def -keywords]
If [const 0] or unset, events are written irrespective of keywords. Otherwise,
only events that have a keyword included in the bitmask are written to the
channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.keywords].

[opt_def -latency]
Milliseconds to wait before flushing buffers. If 0, buffers are flushed only
when full.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.latency].

[opt_def -level]
The severity level threshold for writing events to the channel. Events with
severity values above this are ignored. If [const 0] or unset (empty), all
events are written to the channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.level].

[opt_def -logfilepath]
Path of the backing log file.

[opt_def -logmaxsize]
Maximum size of the log file.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.maxSize].

[opt_def -logretention]
[const 1] or [const 0] depending on whether the backing log file is sequential
or circular.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelloggingtype-complextype ChannelLoggingType.retention].

[opt_def -maxbuffers]
Maximum number of buffers to allocate for the channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.maxBuffers].

[opt_def -minbuffers]
Minimum number of buffers to allocate for the channel.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.minBuffers].

[opt_def -publisher]
Name of the publisher defining the channel.

[opt_def -publisherlist]
List of publishers logging to the channel including the owning publisher that
defined the channel as well as those importing the channel.

[opt_def -sidtype]
[const 1] or [const 0] indicating whether the SID of the logging principal
should be included in events logged to the channel or not.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channelpublishingtype-complextype ChannelPublishingType.sidType].

[opt_def -type] One of [const 0] (Admin), [const 1] (Operational), [const 2]
(Analytic) or [const 3] (Debug).
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/eventmanifestschema-channeltype-complextype ChannelType.type].

[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_channel_config_save] [arg HCHANCFG]]
Saves any changes made to the channel configuration.

[call [cmd evt_channel_config_set] [arg HCHANCFG] [arg PROPERTY] [arg VALUE]]

Sets the value of a configuration property [arg PROPERTY] for the channel
configuration specified by the handle [arg HCHANCFG] to [arg VALUE].
The modification does not take effect until the configuration is saved
with [uri #evt_channel_config_save [cmd evt_channel_config_save]].
See [uri #evt_channel_config_open [cmd evt_channel_config_open]] for
a list of properties.

[call [cmd evt_channels] [opt [arg HSESSION]]]
Returns a list of names of the EVT channels through the EVT session handle
[arg HSESSION]. Defaults to the local system.

[call [cmd evt_clear_log] [arg CHANNELNAME] [opt [arg options]]]
Clears the specified event channel of all events.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -backup] [arg BACKUPFILE]]
Specifies a backup file in which to save events before the event channel
is cleared.
[opt_def [cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]]
Specifies the EVT session handle. Defaults to the local system.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_close] [opt "[arg EVTHANDLE] [arg EVTHANDLE]..."]]
Close handles returned by various EVT commands such as
[uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]] or [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]].

[call [cmd evt_event_decode] [arg HEVENT] [opt [arg options]]]

Returns a Tcl dictionary containing the system-defined event properties,
publisher-defined event data, and formatted message strings.
[arg HEVENT] is a handle to an event such as an element of the list
returned from [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]].

[nl]

The keys in the returned dictionary are the same as the fields of the record
array returned by [uri #evt_event_decode_list [cmd evt_event_decode_list]]. See the
documentation of that command for fields and options.

[nl]
Besides the options for [cmd evt_event_decode_list], the
following additional options may also be specified:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -hpublisher] [arg HPUBLISHER]]
A handle to the event publisher metadata. If not specified, the publisher
metadata for the event is extracted from the event itself.
[opt_def [cmd -values] [arg HVALUES]]
By default, the command will pick any values to be inserted into the
formatted event string from the event itself. If this option is specified,
the values will instead be picked from [arg HVALUES] which must
be a handle to a render values block.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_event_decode_list] [arg HEVENTLIST] [opt [arg options]]]
Returns a [uri base.html#recordarrays "record array"] containing
decoded events. [arg HEVENTLIST] is a list of handles
to events as returned from [uri #evt_next [cmd evt_next]].
[nl]
The returned record array always contains the following system fields
which are present in all events:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -activityid]]
GUID containing the activity id associated with the event.
[opt_def [cmd -channel]]
The channel to which the event was logged.
[opt_def [cmd -computer]]
The name of the system on which the event was logged.
[opt_def [cmd -eventid]]
The event id for the event's definition.
[opt_def [cmd -eventrecordid]]
The id of this event record.
[opt_def [cmd -keywordmask]]
An integer mask with bits corresponding to the event's keywords.
[opt_def [cmd -level]]
The numeric level for the event.
[opt_def [cmd -opcode]]
The numeric opcode for the event.
[opt_def [cmd -pid]]
The process id of the process that logged the event.
[opt_def [cmd -providerguid]]
The GUID of the logging event provider.
[opt_def [cmd -providername]]
The name of the logging event provider.
[opt_def [cmd -qualifiers]]
[opt_def [cmd -relatedactivityid]]
GUID containing the relative activity id associated with the event.
[opt_def [cmd -task]]
The numeric task for the event.
[opt_def [cmd -tid]]
The thread id of the logging thread.
[opt_def [cmd -timecreated]]
The time the event was logged in 100ns units since January 1, 1601 UTC.
[opt_def [cmd -sid]]
The SID of the account under which the logging thread was running.
[opt_def [cmd -version]]
The version of the event definition.
[list_end]

In addition, the returned record array will include the following keys
if they are specified as options to the command:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -keywords]]
Contains a list of keywords with which the event is tagged.
[opt_def [cmd -levelname]]
Contains the string associated by the publisher with the numeric level.
[opt_def [cmd -message]]
Contains the formatted message corresponding to the event.
[opt_def [cmd -opcodename]]
Contains the string associated by the publisher with the numeric opcode field.
[opt_def [cmd -taskname]]
Contains the string associated by the publisher with the numeric task field.
[opt_def [cmd -xml]]
Contains an XML fragment describing the event.
[list_end]

[nl]
The following additional options may also be specified with the command:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -ignorestring] [arg STRING]]
If specified, [arg STRING] is returned for any field that cannot be obtained.
Without this option, errors retrieving fields will generate a Tcl exception.
[opt_def [cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]]
Specifies the language in which the message is to be formatted. Defaults
to the locale of the calling thread. This option is ignored if the
[cmd -hpublisher] option is specified.
[opt_def [cmd -logfile] [arg PATH]]
Specifies the path to the logfile containing archived publisher metadata
for the event.
This option is ignored if the [cmd -hpublisher] option is specified.
[opt_def [cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]]
Specifies the EVT session handle. Defaults to the local system.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_event_logpath] [arg HEVT]]
Returns the path of the channel or file from which the event
identified by [arg HEVT] was retrieved.

[call [cmd evt_event_message] [arg HPUB] [arg HEVT]]
TBD

[call [cmd evt_event_render] [arg HEVENT] [arg HCTX]]
Returns a list of field values from event referenced by [arg HEVENT]
as per the rendering context referenced by [arg HCTX] which must be
a handle returned by
[uri #evt_render_context_xpaths [cmd evt_render_context_xpaths]],
[uri #evt_render_context_userdata [cmd evt_render_context_userdata]], or
[uri #evt_render_context_system [cmd evt_render_context_system]].
The order of values in the first two cases is the order of the XPATHS
in the render context and order of event's user data fields respectively. In
the third case, the order is always that of the
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_system_property_id "EVT_SYSTEM_PROPERTY_ID"]
enumeration in the Windows SDK.
This order can also be retrieved with the
[uri #evt_system_properties [cmd evt_system_properties]] command.

[call [cmd evt_event_xml] [arg HEVENT]]

Returns the XML representation of the event referenced by [arg HEVENT],
including including system-defined metadata and provider-defined event data.
Localized message text is not included but can be retrieved with
[uri #evt_format_message [cmd evt_format_message]]. Alternatively, use the
the [cmd -xml] option to [uri #evt_event_decode [cmd evt_event_decode]]
as it includes localized fields names and formatted messages.

[call [cmd evt_export_log] [arg OUTFILE] [opt [arg options]]]
Exports events, optionally matching a query,
from a channel or a previously exported log file to a target log file.
[nl]
The following options may be specified to select which events are
included in the result set.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -channel] [arg CHANNELNAME]]
Specifies the event channel from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -file] option.
[opt_def [cmd -file] [arg PATH]]
Specifies the event file from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -channel] option.
[opt_def [cmd -ignorequeryerrors]]
Normally, if retrieving a specific event results in an error, Windows
will fail the query and a Tcl exception will be raised.
If [cmd -ignorequeryerrors] is specified, such events are simply skipped
and any further events are returned.
[opt_def [cmd -query] [arg QUERYSTRING]]
Specifies the selection criteria for events that should be included in the
returned result set.
[arg QUERYSTRING] should be [const *] (for all events, default),
an XPATH expression or a structured XML query. See
the Windows SDK for details.
[opt_def [cmd -session] [arg HSESSION]]
Specifies the EVT session handle. Defaults to the local system.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_next] [arg HEVTRESULTSET] [opt [arg options]]]
Returns a list containing events from a event query result set.
[arg HEVTRESULTSET] is a handle to a result set as returned by
[uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]]. The returned list elements
are event handles which can be passed to
[uri #evt_event_decode [cmd evt_event_decode]]. These event handles
must be closed via [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]].
[nl]
The command returns an empty list if no more events are available.
The behaviour of further calls depends on whether [arg HEVTRESULTSET]
was returned from [uri #evt_query [cmd evt_query]] or
[uri #evt_subscribe [cmd evt_subscribe]]. In the former case, an
empty list will continue to be returned even if additional events
are logged to a channel. In the latter case, the command will return
those events.
[nl]
The cursor in the event result set is moved past the returned events
so each [cmd evt_next] returns events from the result set in sequence.
[nl]
The following options can be specified:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -count] [arg COUNT]]
Returns [arg COUNT] events from the result set. The actual number of
events returned may be less if the end of the result set is reached.
If unspecified, the number of returned events depends on various
internal tuning factors and applications must not rely on a specific
number being returned.
[opt_def [cmd -status] [arg VARNAME]]
The name of a variable in which to return status. If there is an
error when reading the next event, the command will normally generate
an Tcl exception. If this option is specified, the command will instead
return an empty list and store the error code in the specified variable.
[opt_def [cmd -timeout] [arg MILLISECONDS]]
Specifies the number of milliseconds to wait for events. A value of
[const -1] (default) indicates call should not time out.
[list_end]

[call [cmd evt_publisher_events] [arg HPUB] [arg PROPNAMES]]

Returns a list containing the metadata for each event defined by the
publisher identified by the handle [arg HPUB]. Each event's metadata
includes the event's metadata properties as a dictionary keyed by
the event property names passed in [arg PROPNAMES]. These correspond
to the [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EVT_EVENT_METADATA_PROPERTY_ID]
values shown in the table below.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -channel]
EventMetadataEventChannel
[opt_def -id]
EventMetadataEventID
[opt_def -keyword]
EventMetadataEventKeyword
[opt_def -level]
EventMetadataEventLevel
[opt_def -messageid]
EventMetadataEventMessageID
[opt_def -opcode]
EventMetadataEventOpcode
